---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import PageToc from '../../components/PageToc.astro';
import SectionSearch from '../../components/SectionSearch.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../../consts';

const notebookUrl =
	'https://github.com/rangarajp/rangarajp.github.io/blob/main/notebooks/LLM_Scratch/transformers.ipynb';
const notebookDir =
	'https://github.com/rangarajp/rangarajp.github.io/tree/main/notebooks/LLM_Scratch';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`GPT from Scratch - ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="Build a GPT-style decoder-only Transformer from scratch in PyTorch — tokenizer through training on The Verdict."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<div class="handbook-shell bfs-shell">
			<main class="handbook-main">
				<article class="prose">
					<SectionSearch />
					<header class="article-header">
						<nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
							<a href="/build-from-scratch">Build from Scratch</a>
							<span class="separator">/</span>
							<span aria-current="page">GPT</span>
						</nav>
						<a href="/build-from-scratch" class="back-link">&larr; Build from Scratch</a>
						<h1>GPT from Scratch</h1>
						<p class="description">
							A decoder-only Transformer in PyTorch: build each piece, smoke-test with tiny
							tensors, then train on <em>The Verdict</em>.
						</p>
					</header>

				<p>
					Theory lives under
					<a href="/concepts/transformers">Concepts → Transformers</a>. This page walks the
					runnable notebook in
					<a href={notebookDir}><code>notebooks/LLM_Scratch/</code></a>. For the
					encoder–decoder seq2seq build, see
					<a href="/build-from-scratch/transformers">Transformer from Scratch</a>.
				</p>

				<p>
					<em>Flow:</em> tokenizer → dataset → embeddings → attention → block → GPT →
					generate → train.
				</p>

				<h2>What you build</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>GPT-2 BPE tokenization via <code>tiktoken</code></li>
					<li>Sliding-window next-token dataset and DataLoader</li>
					<li>
						Causal multi-head attention, LayerNorm, GELU feed-forward, and a stacked GPT
						model
					</li>
					<li>Greedy text generation and a simple AdamW training loop on <em>The Verdict</em></li>
				</ul>

				<h2>1. Config</h2>
				<p>
					Shared hyperparameters used by every module (short context for fast training):
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><code>vocab_size=50257</code> (GPT-2 BPE)</li>
					<li><code>context_length=256</code>, <code>emb_dim=768</code></li>
					<li><code>n_heads=12</code>, <code>n_layers=12</code></li>
					<li><code>drop_rate=0.1</code>, <code>qkv_bias=False</code></li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					That is a GPT-2–small–shaped stack with a shorter context window so CPU/GPU
					smoke runs stay practical.
				</p>

				<h2>2. Tokenizer and dataset</h2>
				<p>
					Encode text to integer IDs with GPT-2 BPE, then cut a sliding window over the
					token stream:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><em>Input</em> — tokens <code>[t0 … tN−1]</code></li>
					<li><em>Target</em> — next-token shift <code>[t1 … tN]</code></li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					A tiny string smoke test checks that <code>inputs[:, 1:]</code> equals
					<code>targets[:, :-1]</code> before any model code runs.
				</p>

				<h2>3. Embeddings</h2>
				<p>
					Each token ID and each position becomes a vector; their sum is the model input:
				</p>
				<pre><code>token_embeddings + pos_embeddings → (batch, seq, emb_dim)</code></pre>

				<h2>4. Attention stack</h2>
				<p>Attention is built in three steps so shapes stay obvious:</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<em>Self-attention</em> — single head, no batch, no mask yet
						(<code>Q Kᵀ / √d</code>, softmax, times <code>V</code>)
					</li>
					<li>
						<em>Causal attention</em> — batched, with an upper-triangular mask so token
						<em>i</em> cannot attend to future tokens
					</li>
					<li>
						<em>Multi-head attention</em> — split embedding into <em>h</em> heads, attend
						per head, concat, project with <code>W_o</code>
					</li>
				</ol>
				<pre><code>(B, S, d) → Q,K,V → (B, h, S, d/h) → scores → weights @ V → (B, S, d) → W_o</code></pre>

				<h2>5. Block and GPT model</h2>
				<p>Each transformer block is pre-norm with residuals:</p>
				<pre><code>x → LayerNorm → MHA → dropout → +x
  → LayerNorm → FFN (4× emb, GELU) → dropout → +x</code></pre>
				<p>
					<code>GPTModel</code> stacks token + position embeddings, <em>N</em> blocks,
					final LayerNorm, then a linear head to vocab logits
					<code>(batch, seq, vocab_size)</code>.
				</p>

				<h2>6. Generation and training</h2>
				<p>
					Greedy decode takes the last-position logits, argmax, appends, and repeats —
					untrained samples are nonsense by design. Training uses cross-entropy over the
					next-token shift on <em>The Verdict</em> (downloaded if missing), AdamW
					(<code>lr=4e-4</code>), and periodic eval plus a printed sample each epoch.
				</p>
				<p>
					Untrained loss should sit near <code>ln(vocab) ≈ 10.8</code>. After a few epochs
					loss drops and samples start echoing the story’s style.
				</p>
				<pre><code>cd notebooks/LLM_Scratch
# open transformers.ipynb and run top to bottom</code></pre>

				<h2>Code index</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<a href={notebookUrl}>transformers.ipynb</a> — full walkthrough (all sections
						above)
					</li>
					<li>
						<a href={`${notebookDir}/the-verdict.txt`}>the-verdict.txt</a> — training text
						(or auto-downloaded)
					</li>
				</ul>

				<p>
					For the mental model behind each block, start at
					<a href="/concepts/transformers">Transformers Overview</a>. For serving-side math
					on matmul FLOPs and attention cost, see
					<a href="/concepts/llm-inference/inference-engineering-mathematics">Inference Engineering Mathematics</a>.
				</p>
			</article>
			</main>
			<PageToc />
		</div>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

